# AGENTS.md

## Purpose

Repository rules for the `{{ appKey }}` Nexia App Package.

## Host boundary

- Work only in this App repository. Do not copy, mount, edit or deploy Core,
  Sandbox, CLI or SDK source/runtime images. The platform operator owns them.
- Import PHP platform contracts from `nexia-cloud-os/sdk-laravel` and
  frontend contracts from `@nexia/sdk`. Do not import Core or another App.
- Preserve the Composer PSR-4 namespace and registered App identity. Use
  Resource References, public contracts and declared events for cross-App work.
- Use `nexia login` and project `nexia dev` for the operator-managed Sandbox. Keep
  secrets and server code outside public/. Never request login tokens in chat.
- Keep required installation data in this App's initializer; sample data is
  separate. Do not run other Apps' seeders or edit their migrations.
- App Family and launcher order are review requests; central administrators
  approve the catalog presentation.
- Report missing SDK/platform capabilities through developer-support. Prepare
  sanitized reports for developer review before submission.

## Read before implementation

- Read the public SDK guide and permission guide before changing platform calls:
  https://developers.nexia.to/docs/app-sdk-sdk-contracts
  https://developers.nexia.to/docs/architecture-permissions-and-roles
- For an authenticated Laravel request use `$request->user()->getAuthIdentifier()`.
  For `Nexia\Identity\Contracts\Actor` use `$actor->key()`. Never assume the
  actor is a Core Eloquent model. App-owned model records may still use `getKey()`.
- Match frontend permission queries to the Resource scope. A tenant permission
  result cannot replace Legal Entity or Operating Unit authorization. Keep the
  backend Policy authoritative and distinguish loading from denied access.
- After saving, show the successful API result or invalidate the relevant query.
  An uncertain response is not a failed write: use the host save-result recovery
  before retrying. Do not implement automatic duplicate submissions.
- At validation time run `nexia check` first, then verify one permitted and one
  denied operation and the saved result in the sandbox. Static checks do not
  prove runtime permissions or persistence. Report the file, command, sanitized
  error and what remains unverified; do not patch host internals to make a test pass.

## Organization scope UI

- Put page-owned organization scope in the Route Surface `organizationScope`
  slot through the SDK `NxOrganizationTargetSelector`; it renders after the
  breadcrumb and before header actions, and is not an action. Do
  not repeat it in a ResourceTable toolbar or `meta.list_schema.filterable`
  flyout.
- Generated standard common-data profiles render no selector. A tenant-owned
  custom relation or Operating Unit page may declare one. The Route Surface
  explicitly chooses its supported axes and read permission; ownership,
  applicability, and permission scope are distinct. Never infer scope from
  columns.
- Default an ordinary list to `useOrganizationListScope` with its read
  permission. Custom relationship or multi-permission pages keep their own URL
  adapter; do not force those authorities into the shared hook.
- Keep status, type, date, and other record conditions as ResourceTable filters.
- Compose forms with public NxFormSection/NxFormField inputs and the shared
  draft binding. Use NxResourceFormLegalEntityField for an authorized create
  target or the stored owner's human label on edit. Keep read content as
  text/links/badges, not disabled inputs. RecordSurface owns read/create/edit
  modes; do not infer editing authority from the route or hide a mounted form.
  Do not add a frontend Information Schema or duplicate owner selectors.


## Frontend build performance

- Use `nexiaAppConfig(import.meta.url)` from `@nexia/sdk/vite` with build SDK
  0.8.2 or later. Keep route/widget surfaces lazy and host-provided peers external.
- Extend the shared configuration instead of replacing its build contract. Keep
  relative asset URLs, dependency preloading and the host compatibility manifest;
  Vite library mode disables the default lazy dependency preloading.
- Rebuild and publish after upgrading the build SDK; assembling a shared runtime
  image does not rebuild reviewed frontend assets. Existing lockfiles must resolve
  the upgraded SDK, not merely declare a compatible range.
- Check the built entry and lazy dependency graph, then measure first entry,
  within-App and cross-App navigation separately. A successful build or a visible
  heading does not prove that business data is ready. Do not eagerly bundle every
  screen or cache permission decisions to hide request overhead.

## Query performance

- Keep App-owned SQL and business dependency checks in the App; report shared
  SDK/host overhead to the platform owner instead of bypassing its checks.
- Authorize and filter before pagination. For list enrichment, batch only the
  authorized page's IDs with eager loading, aggregates or EXISTS as appropriate;
  do not add SQL or host callbacks per row in serializers. Use existing public
  batch contracts when available; never query Core or another App's tables.
- Host identities are SDK interfaces, not Eloquent models. Use `Party::publicId()` /
  `displayLabel()`; use `BatchPersonProfileDirectory` for page-wide person profile
  fields such as `legalName`. Test against interface-only doubles and distinct
  profiles so raw model properties and per-row lookups fail in local checks.
- For status/count flags, use bounded aggregates or EXISTS instead of hydrating
  all child records. Test date boundaries and lifecycle precedence against detail results.
- Measure full pages with skewed child counts. If correlated EXISTS repeatedly
  scans a child table, group flags once for the authorized page IDs.
- Preserve tenant/actor/organization scope and soft-delete semantics. List flags
  are display results, not write authority: mutations recheck current permission
  and dependencies. Never cache actor decisions across requests to gain speed.
- At finalization, cover several distinct rows, empty results and changed
  dependencies; assert query/callback growth stays bounded for the changed path.
  Compare the same data, actor scope and page size; distinguish cold/warm runs,
  App SQL, host callbacks, full HTTP and browser time. See
  https://developers.nexia.to/docs/building-apps-test-an-app for measurement guidance.

## Safety

- Tenant data and execution remain isolated.
- Authentication does not grant authorization; protected operations are
  enforced in the backend.
- Async work restores the tenant and actor context it requires.
- Durable product state does not depend only on browser storage.

Draft implementation and follow-up revisions defer automated validation. When
the user requests testing, verification, finalization, a commit, a push, or a
pull request, run the smallest focused validation that can disprove the changed
risk once. Reuse successful evidence while its relevant scope is unchanged.
Do not commit, push, publish, or mutate external services without explicit user
authorization.

## Change delivery

Use a purpose-named work branch in each changed repository. Do not create
change commits on, or push directly to, `main` or `develop`. Deliver through a
PR/MR and describe related Core/SDK/App changes and their merge order. Creating
a PR/MR does not authorize merging it; merge requires explicit user approval.
Do not weaken branch protection. Core, SDK, and App developers follow the same
workflow; no developer-specific local policy file is required.

For exact-self Resource References, opt into `ResourceReferenceSelfServiceContribution` only when every supported read method restricts records to the restored actor and organization. Admit explicit purposes; test other actors, organizations, forged IDs/filters/dates and revoked source authority. Never infer self authority from a request body flag. See the Resource References developer guide.
